Feature: a tap on a demuxer, for a copy of the media made without decoding.

An embedder that saves what a page plays needs the encoded buffers its
decoders get, with the configuration they need, whatever the page
delivers: a file the element loads (FFmpegDemuxer), what a player script
appends through Media Source Extensions (ChunkDemuxer), or HLS the engine
plays itself (ManifestDemuxer, a ChunkDemuxer inside). A new
media::DemuxerTap gets each stream's AudioDecoderConfig or
VideoDecoderConfig before its buffers and again when it changes, and the
buffers in decode order, with their decode timestamps where the demuxer
has them (StreamParserBuffer's), on whichever thread the demuxer works on.

Demuxer::SetTap(tap, from) starts it, with what the demuxer holds already:
FFmpegDemuxer's queued buffers (about the playhead on), the
SourceBufferStream's ranges from the keyframe at or before `from` on (or
all of them), then what it demuxes or what is appended. Other demuxers do
nothing. DemuxerManager gives its demuxer to the player.

DemuxerTap::KeepForTapsFromNow() says taps may come in the process (the
embedder looks for media to copy): from then on FFmpegDemuxer keeps the
video its decoders took since the last keyframe (up to 16 MiB), so that a
tap starts at the keyframe of what is shown. Until then a demuxer keeps
nothing for a tap, and costs nothing more.

